</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the local extrema of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^3-3x.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine the concavity of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^3-6x^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the inflection points of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^3-6x^2.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State Rolle's Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>State the Mean Value Theorem.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify that</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>satisfies the hypotheses of the Mean Value Theorem on <m>[1,4]</m>, and find
the corresponding value of <m>c</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A sphere has radius increasing at <m>3</m> cm/s. Find the rate at which its
volume changes when the radius is <m>4</m> cm.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A rectangle has area <m>100</m>. Find the dimensions minimizing its perimeter.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate using L'Hospital's Rule:</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to0}\frac{e^x-1}{x}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\int(4x^3-6x+2)\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find</p>

<p>
<md>\int\left(e^x+\cos x\right)\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Solve</p>

<p>
<md>F'(x)=3x^2,\qquad F(2)=10.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\int_0^3 2x\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\int_1^4\left(x^2+\frac1x\right)\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\int 6x(x^2+4)^5\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Evaluate</p>

<p>
<md>\int_0^1 2xe^{x^2}\,dx.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area bounded by</p>

<p>
<md>y=x</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>y=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>on <m>[0,1]</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the average value of</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>on <m>[0,3]</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A particle has velocity</p>

<p>
<md>v(t)=3t^2-6t.</md>
</p>

<p>Find its displacement from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=3</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For the previous velocity function, find total distance traveled on
<m>[0,3]</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume formed by rotating</p>

<p>
<md>y=x</md>
</p>

<p>on <m>[0,2]</m> around the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume formed by rotating the region between</p>

<p>
<md>y=2</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>y=x</md>
</p>

<p>on <m>[0,2]</m> around the <m>x</m>-axis using washers.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the arc length of</p>

<p>
<md>y=\frac23x^{3/2}</md>
</p>

<p>from <m>x=0</m> to <m>x=3</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>\int_1^\infty\frac1{x^3}\,dx</md>
</p>

<p>converges, and evaluate it if it does.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>f(x)=x^4-4x^2,</md>
</p>

<p>determine:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>critical numbers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>increasing and decreasing intervals;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>local extrema;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>concavity;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>inflection points.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A rectangular enclosure is built against a river, so fencing is required
on only three sides. If <m>200</m> meters of fencing are available, determine
the dimensions maximizing the enclosed area.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Let</p>

<p>
<md>F(x)=\int_1^{x^2}\sqrt{1+t^3}\,dt.</md>
</p>

<p>Find <m>F'(x)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-02-single-variable-calculus-subsec-single-variable-summary">
<title>Conceptual Summary</title>
<p>Single-variable calculus is organized around three interacting ideas.</p>

<p>First, limits describe local and long-term behavior:</p>

<p>
<md>\lim_{x\to a}f(x).</md>
</p>

<p>Second, derivatives describe instantaneous change:</p>

<p>
<md>f'(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.</md>
</p>

<p>Third, integrals describe accumulation:</p>

<p>
<md>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx.</md>
</p>

<p>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus connects differentiation and integration:</p>

<p>
<md>\dv{}{x}\int_a^x f(t)\,dt=f(x),</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx=F(b)-F(a).</md>
</p>

<p>\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=12mm,
    box/.style={draw,rounded corners,align=center,inner sep=6pt},
    &gt;=stealth
]
\node[box] (local) {Local behavior};
\node[box,below=of local] (limit) {Limits};
\node[box,below left=of limit] (deriv) {Derivatives};
\node[box,below right=of limit] (integral) {Integrals};
\node[box,below=18mm of $(deriv)!0.5!(integral)$] (ftc)
{Fundamental Theorem\\of Calculus};</p>

<p>\draw[-&gt;] (local)--(limit);
\draw[-&gt;] (limit)--(deriv);
\draw[-&gt;] (limit)--(integral);
\draw[&lt;-&gt;,thick] (deriv)--(integral);
\draw[-&gt;] (deriv)--(ftc);
\draw[-&gt;] (integral)--(ftc);
\end{tikzpicture}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-02-single-variable-calculus-subsec-single-variable-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Single-variable calculus develops the first systematic mathematical theory
of continuous change. Limits provide the foundation for continuity and
instantaneous behavior. Derivatives measure tangent slopes and rates of
change and lead to the Mean Value Theorem, optimization, curve analysis,
approximation, and related rates. Antiderivatives reverse differentiation,
while definite integrals describe signed accumulation through limits of
Riemann sums. The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus establishes the central
inverse relationship between derivatives and integrals. These ideas become
the foundation for multivariable calculus, vector calculus, differential
equations, probability, numerical methods, and the rigorous theories of
limits, continuity, differentiation, and integration developed later in
Real Analysis.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
